4. THE REGISTERED DECISION — does the error-volume ranking differ from AUC's?
================================================================================================================
   Error volumes: LOWER is better. AUC: HIGHER is better. Both descriptive arm means over
   the cell's 400 rows; no n and no p is attached to a ranking (F4).
   `prev` = true_frac_above_tau, carried beside every row because a ranking read without
   its prevalence inverts. NOTE the gamma direction: gamma enters tau multiplicatively
   through tau_max, which DECREASES in gamma, so HIGH gamma means a LOW absolute
   threshold and a HUGE positive class. At gamma=0.99, tau_frac=0.60 prevalence is 0.9992 —
   the minority class there is the NEGATIVE one, ~16 of 20,000 points (Erratum 3).

   Spearman rho against the AUC ordering: +1 identical, -1 exactly reversed.
   `tie` = every arm scores identically, so there is no ranking to compare.

